Chapter: VehicleLimits
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Trig

 What a vehicle on this route may do: how hard it accelerates, how fast it will go,
 how early it starts thinking about the intersection, and how fast it dares take a
 corner.

 THE RIDER AND THE TRUCK BOTH READ THESE, so they belong to neither. The truck
 expresses its whole envelope as a multiple of this one -- `truck-chase-accel` is
 `1.1 * a-accel` and `truck-max-v` is `1.1 * v-max` -- which is the sense in which
 it chases: it is the same vehicle with ten percent more of everything. Before this
 chapter existed those four names lived in Rider and Truck cited Rider for them,
 which read as the chased depending on the chaser.

 LIMITS, not physics. These are an envelope the two vehicles are held inside and
 nothing integrates here; the numbers are chosen to look right rather than derived
 from anything. The integration that actually moves a bike is
 `Safari chapter Bike`.

 We say:

Section: The Envelope

  a-accel : Real
  a-accel = 0.010

  v-max : Real
  v-max = 2.5

  approach-intersection-dist : Real
  approach-intersection-dist = 60.0

Section: Corner Entry Speed

 The safe entry speed for a turn angle: the table from the TypeScript, and every
 angle on this route is tabulated. The comparison is on the ROUNDED degrees, which
 is what makes an exact match on a Real safe here.

  turn-speed : Real -> Real
  turn-speed (angle-rad) =
    let d = round-real (angle-rad * 180.0 / pi)
    in if d ~0 15.0 then 1.297
    else if d ~0 20.0 then 0.840
    else if d ~0 30.0 then 0.461
    else if d ~0 50.0 then 0.222
    else if d ~0 70.0 then 0.139
    else if d ~0 80.0 then 0.117
    else 0.222
